
This directory is intended for project header files.

A header file is a file containing C declarations and macro definitions
to be shared between several project source files. You request the use of a
header file in your project source file (C, C++, etc) located in `src` folder
by including it, with the C preprocessing directive `#include'.

```src/main.c

#include "header.h"

int main (void)
{
 ...
}
```

Including a header file produces the same results as copying the header file
into each source file that needs it. Such copying would be time-consuming
and error-prone. With a header file, the related declarations appear
in only one place. If they need to be changed, they can be changed in one
place, and programs that include the header file will automatically use the
new version when next recompiled. The header file eliminates the labor of
finding and changing all the copies as well as the risk that a failure to
find one copy will result in inconsistencies within a program.

In C, the convention is to give header files names that end with `.h'.

Read more about using header files in official GCC documentation:

* Include Syntax
* Include Operation
* Once-Only Headers
* Computed Includes

https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/cpp/Header-Files.html






//Brownout  Troubleshooting
## ESP32-CAM Brownout Troubleshooting

### Problem

After uploading the firmware, the ESP32-CAM repeatedly restarted and the
Serial Monitor displayed:

    Brownout detector was triggered

### Cause

A brownout occurs when the ESP32 detects that its supply voltage has fallen
below a safe operating level.

In this setup, the ESP32-CAM was powered through the FTDI adapter's 5V
output. Although the voltage was set to 5V, the FTDI may not provide enough
current for the ESP32-CAM, particularly during startup and Wi-Fi operation.

### Boot sequence

ESP32-CAM
    ↓
Boot
    ↓
PSRAM initialization
    ↓
Higher power/current demand
    ↓
Supply voltage drops
    ↓
Brownout detector
    ↓
ESP32 reset
    ↓
Boot repeats

### Diagnosis

The following were verified:

- PlatformIO build succeeded
- AI Thinker ESP32-CAM was detected
- Firmware upload completed successfully
- FTDI was detected on COM3
- Serial Monitor received ESP32 boot messages
- HC-SR04 was disconnected during testing

Therefore, the problem was related to the power supply rather than
firmware compilation, uploading, or the ultrasonic sensor.

### Solution

Use a stable regulated 5V power source capable of supplying sufficient
current for the ESP32-CAM.

The FTDI can be used for USB-to-serial communication while the ESP32-CAM
receives power from an appropriate supply.

Do not connect two independent 5V sources together.

Always ensure that the ESP32-CAM and FTDI have a common GND when the FTDI
is being used for serial communication.

### Important

Correct voltage does not necessarily mean sufficient current. An ESP32-CAM
can require significantly more current during startup and Wi-Fi activity,
so an inadequate USB-to-serial adapter power output can cause repeated
brownout resets.

connection                  ESP32-CAM
              ┌─────────────┐
External +5V ─┤ 5V          │
External GND ─┤ GND         │
              │             │
FTDI TX ──────┤ U0R         │
FTDI RX ──────┤ U0T         │
FTDI GND ─────┤ GND         │
              └─────────────┘

              i guess use 5v h+ battery